{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Re-check of AC5 (fsck triage) and the Sep \"stale-cache\" false refusal, 2026-11-03 PST, against origin/main f1fe7c6f587. Investigation only: production was read only, no repair, no force.\n\n## A. fsck (MCP `fsck` recover=false at 09:31 PST; read-only SSM on the server clone)\n\nServer clone `/var/gerrit/site/mcp-tickets`: HEAD == origin/tickets, 0/0. Rows today compared with the 2026-10-18 list:\n- `diverged`: 0. Gone.\n- `env_id_mismatch`: 1 on the server (own env eea68b7f, server `.env-id` dated 2026-08-26 PDT; foreign envs ad49f17f and b2b5c498). The local fsck also reports 1, with different ids (own 492a5c71; foreign ade0f2ef and b8a354e0). This is the row as it reads today, while disruptive-breezy-esok is changing it.\n- `unsigned_env`: 0, both on the server and locally.\n- `tracker_dirty_tmp_event`: now **2** on the server: `.tmp-event-ozn20x_n` (SIGNATURE uuid e5aea5b2, staged 2026-09-03 13:56 PDT) and a new `.tmp-event-5hczbnpq` (COMPLETION_VERDICT for bea4-b9cc-18cc-40c4, uuid 30f74a79, staged 2026-10-23 22:37:46 PDT). Neither uuid is on origin/tickets. bea4-b9cc has a different COMPLETION_VERDICT (edbcd790) that landed at 22:57 PDT. The new staging file was written 12 s after autodeploy began a blue-green MCP deploy (22:37:34 PDT).\n- 143f-b548 second SNAPSHOT `1792380009447157001-49b39ec6`: renamed to `.retired` at 2026-10-18 22:23 PDT. The 12 rows on 143f-b548 are gone (0).\n- `snapshot_inconsistent` 15 + `orphan_event` 8 on the server, 0 locally. All 23 rows come from 23 **untracked** files in the server working tree (`git status` `??`). Each is a pre-compaction event file that has a tracked `.retired` sibling. All 23 have the same mtime, 2026-09-04 22:23:46 PDT. On origin/tickets these paths were renamed to `.retired` by rebar-bridge[bot] COMPACT commits at 23:19 PDT that night. The server and local projections of aa9e-3d35, 7147-6ea7 and d82c-decc are identical (status, comments, deps), so these leftovers do not change what readers see.\n- The local `rebar store fsck --dry-run` differs from the server only in: the env ids above; the 23 untracked-file rows; `tracker_dirty_tmp_event`; and a transient `push_pending` (1 commit) on the server. Both report the same 16 `status_fork_resolved` and 3 `uncertified_close` rows.\n\nHypotheses (unproven): (1) the 2 staging files are writes abandoned when the writing process ended between staging and publishing; the timing of the Oct one fits a container stop during a deploy. (2) The 23 leftovers were written by one bulk checkout or restore at 22:23:46 PDT, and the later adopt of the rename did not remove them because the clone did not track them at that point.\n\n## B. \"2 commit(s) BEHIND\" false refusal (H1 server-clone lag vs H2 in-process cache)\n\nExperiment: one local-CLI probe comment, committed 09:51:28 PST. A host loop sampled the server clone every 3 s for 255 s. The MCP `show_ticket` and `plan_review_status` reads were polled every 8 s.\n- At 09:51:32 the clone was still at the old HEAD. FETCH_HEAD was touched at 09:51:33 and 09:51:38. HEAD advanced to 7d9a047c96 at 09:51:39 (reflog `reset: moving to origin/tickets`).\n- The first MCP `show_ticket` started at 09:51:32, took 10.0 s, and already contained the probe. Later calls took about 2 s. `plan_review_status` read `unsigned`, never `stale-store`. The clone stayed 0/0 in all 85 samples.\n- The MCP read itself ran the fetch and the adopt (the throttle marker was 75 s old). No interval was observed where the clone held the commit and MCP served older state.\n\nCode at origin/main: `store_freshness` (`_store/freshness.py`) is pure on-disk git with no cache. Its \"N commit(s) BEHIND\" means the server clone's HEAD is an ancestor of the server's own already-fetched `origin/tickets`. The in-process caches on the read path are keyed by mtime/size or an immutable tree oid. No time-based state cache exists.\n\nHypothesis (from reading the code, not tested): `sync.reconverge` fetches outside the write lock, then adopts under a 2 s lock with 1 attempt and silently returns if the lock is busy. `ensure_fresh` writes the 60 s throttle marker before it reconverges. The claim gate does not force a refresh (only close passes `force=True`). Together these would leave the clone BEHIND for as long as another holder keeps the lock (for example a compact-sweep), which can be longer than the fetch interval. That fits the Sep report without any cache. The Sep evidence came from the operator's clone, which the gate does not read.\n\nVerdicts: A reproduces in part (tmp-event, env_id_mismatch, and 23 server-only leftover rows; diverged, unsigned_env and the 143f rows are gone). B does not reproduce, and the cache claim is not supported.\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793729204783199251,"uuid":"38072e81-e481-4392-9d6a-f1145babcd4a"}